From 2d61a18d803c5abdee54674c3d80fb36daad7acd Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: NayiemW <37852059+NayiemW@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 25 Sep 2026 19:30:18 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] S1-25: rule-height-block (L-97)

Transaction-level rules for block H read the milestone at H, whether
the block's transactions are decoded (byte or data path), applied,
reverted, or used for events. Pool admission, pool re-adds and the
collator's validation read tip + 1, the height of the block the
transaction is for. Everything else is unchanged; the revoke delay and
the collator's block budget stay as they are.

ConfigManager gets a scoped height: runAtHeight(h, fn) runs fn in an
AsyncLocalStorage scope that getHeight() and getMilestone() (with no
height argument) honour, along fn's own async chain only; outside a
scope nothing changes. The scopes are opened in:
- Blocks.Deserialiser.deserialise: the transaction part, at the block's
  height;
- BlockFactory.fromData and fromSerialised: the body, at the block's
  height;
- BlockState.applyBlock and revertBlock: the body, at H, so the revert
  after a failed transaction also reads H;
- pool Processor.process, Service.readdTransactions (inside its lock)
  and Collator.getBlockCandidateTransactions: at tip + 1. The pool
  worker inherits it through getHeight(). The processor now injects
  the StateStore; the collator uses the tip it already reads.

Most of the diff re-indents the wrapped bodies; git show -w shows the
real change.

Test: patches/tests/25-rule-height-block.test.js (fails on the
unpatched build, passes here).

Patch: 25
Ledger: L-97 (3)
Consensus: yes
